\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The same $500$ held-out problems, solved five other ways. Classical timings are one CPU core and one query; the learned rows are one GPU and $20$ samples per query, so the time column is not a like-for-like comparison. Classical clearance is averaged over solved problems; the learned rows are per-sample rates at $250$ training environments}}{6}{table.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:baselines}{{1}{6}{The same $500$ held-out problems, solved five other ways. Classical timings are one CPU core and one query; the learned rows are one GPU and $20$ samples per query, so the time column is not a like-for-like comparison. Classical clearance is averaged over solved problems; the learned rows are per-sample rates at $250$ training environments}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Where the learned planner actually sits. The world-frame arm lies on the straight-line floor; the reduction lifts it by $30.2$ points; the classical planners remain far above. We show this rather than omit it: the contribution here is the representation result, not a competitive planner}}{6}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:baselines}{{1}{6}{Where the learned planner actually sits. The world-frame arm lies on the straight-line floor; the reduction lifts it by $30.2$ points; the classical planners remain far above. We show this rather than omit it: the contribution here is the representation result, not a competitive planner}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {B}Calibration: floors and ceilings}{6}{subsection.5.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:baselines}{{V-B}{6}{Calibration: floors and ceilings}{subsection.5.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The untrained stochastic baseline: Brownian bridges pinned at the endpoints, $20$ samples per query, same collision check. Widening the prior buys nothing at any setting, so best-of-$N$ over random detours is not what a learned sampler is competing against}}{7}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:prior}{{2}{7}{The untrained stochastic baseline: Brownian bridges pinned at the endpoints, $20$ samples per query, same collision check. Widening the prior buys nothing at any setting, so best-of-$N$ over random detours is not what a learned sampler is competing against}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Held-out performance against training-set size. The control gains $2.6$ points across a $12.5\times $ increase in environments; the reduction gains $26.6$ and has not plateaued. Minimum clearance is an independent, continuous measure and tells the same story}}{7}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:main}{{3}{7}{Held-out performance against training-set size. The control gains $2.6$ points across a $12.5\times $ increase in environments; the reduction gains $26.6$ and has not plateaued. Minimum clearance is an independent, continuous measure and tells the same story}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {C}The reduction scales; the baseline does not}{7}{subsection.5.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The gap widens with data. If canonicalisation were compensating for a shortage of training data one would expect it to close; instead the world-frame baseline is nearly flat while the reduced model continues to improve. The hollow marker is the no-roll ablation, drawn on the treatment line because it is the same arm with one component removed}}{8}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scaling}{{2}{8}{The gap widens with data. If canonicalisation were compensating for a shortage of training data one would expect it to close; instead the world-frame baseline is nearly flat while the reduced model continues to improve. The hollow marker is the no-roll ablation, drawn on the treatment line because it is the same arm with one component removed}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Decomposition by mechanism on the same held-out set. The entire effect is attributable to canonicalisation}}{8}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:ablation}{{4}{8}{Decomposition by mechanism on the same held-out set. The entire effect is attributable to canonicalisation}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {D}The mechanisms for the sixth degree of freedom are inert}{8}{subsection.5.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {E}How much of the level is training budget}{8}{subsection.5.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:budget}{{V-E}{8}{How much of the level is training budget}{subsection.5.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces (a) Only the canonicalisation clears the noise floor; the two mechanisms built for the sixth degree of freedom sit inside it. (b) The non-equivariance residual of Eq.\nobreakspace  {}\eqref  {eq:resid} is smaller at the larger scale --- two points at one seed, so read it as consistent with the property arriving on its own rather than as establishing it; the hollow marker shows that removing roll augmentation raises the residual but leaves it small, so augmentation was not the cause of the near-equivariance}}{9}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:mechanisms}{{3}{9}{(a) Only the canonicalisation clears the noise floor; the two mechanisms built for the sixth degree of freedom sit inside it. (b) The non-equivariance residual of Eq.~\eqref {eq:resid} is smaller at the larger scale --- two points at one seed, so read it as consistent with the property arriving on its own rather than as establishing it; the hollow marker shows that removing roll augmentation raises the residual but leaves it small, so augmentation was not the cause of the near-equivariance}{figure.3}{}}
